WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.116 --> 00:00:14.660
The telegram that Detective Fix sent to Scotland Yard had an instant effect on opinion in London.

2
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.961
Suddenly Phileas Fogg was no longer an eccentric rich man who had accepted a wager to travel around

3
00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:21.441
the world.

4
00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:25.683
Now he was a suspected gentleman bank robber!

5
00:00:25.703 --> 00:00:30.564
Betting on his grand adventure ceased, and his name was dropped from the betting books.

6
00:00:32.072 --> 00:00:38.134
Instead his name filled the papers as speculation grew about his connection to the theft of the fifty-five-thousand

7
00:00:38.174 --> 00:00:40.234
pounds. Mr.

8
00:00:40.294 --> 00:00:44.976
Fogg, oblivious to the telegram or any news at home, was focused just on his goal.

9
00:00:46.216 --> 00:00:51.677
In fact, after breakfast, and while Passepartout was shopping for necessities in Suez, Mr.

10
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:53.758
Fogg went to see the captain of the Mongolia.

11
00:00:53.778 --> 00:00:57.479
"My good sir," began Mr.

12
00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:01.180
Fogg, "may I have a word with you regarding our voyage to Bombay?"

13
00:01:02.268 --> 00:01:06.630
"I can speak with you only briefly, sir, as we are set to sail in less than an

14
00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.111
hour," said the captain.

15
00:01:08.131 --> 00:01:11.573
"What I have to say is brief," replied Mr.

16
00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:17.036
Fogg. "I am in a great hurry and would be obliged if you could reach Bombay in the

17
00:01:17.096 --> 00:01:18.357
shortest time possible.

18
00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:24.800
In fact, I am willing to offer you a considerable sum if you arrive in Bombay more than

19
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.201
a day ahead of schedule."

20
00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:28.742
The captain was surprised by Mr.

21
00:01:28.802 --> 00:01:31.604
Fogg's request but had no trouble agreeing to make haste.

22
00:01:32.716 --> 00:01:36.837
When Passepartout returned to the Mongolia shortly before it set sail, Mr.

23
00:01:36.857 --> 00:01:37.837
Fogg was waiting for him.

24
00:01:40.198 --> 00:01:42.499
Detective Fix was also on board, planning Mr.

25
00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:43.239
Fogg's arrest.

26
00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:46.440
The detective hoped that the warrant for Mr.

27
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.800
Fogg would be ready when the boat arrived in Bombay.

28
00:01:49.781 --> 00:01:53.962
After passing through the famous canal at Suez, the Mongolia entered the Red Sea.

29
00:01:55.922 --> 00:02:01.844
Winds from the African coast made the waves boisterous, and many passengers speedily disappeared below deck.

30
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:08.984
As he watched the wind and the billowing waves, was Phileas Fogg anxious about the steamer's progress?

31
00:02:10.685 --> 00:02:15.107
As calmly as if he were sitting in his Reform Club, he passed through the Red Sea with

32
00:02:15.147 --> 00:02:20.530
indifference. When the weather calmed, he did not view the historic villages on the coast.

33
00:02:21.911 --> 00:02:26.214
The Mongolia ploughed on into the Arabian Sea, and he paid no attention.

34
00:02:27.975 --> 00:02:33.543
He ate four hearty meals a day, slept soundly every night, and passed his time playing whist with

35
00:02:33.663 --> 00:02:35.884
other enthusiastic card players like himself.

36
00:02:37.325 --> 00:02:40.528
Passepartout, however, thoroughly enjoyed watching the scenery.

37
00:02:42.089 --> 00:02:45.992
He spent hours every day lounging on the deck and taking in his surroundings.

38
00:02:47.613 --> 00:02:49.414
He ate his meals in the forward cabin.

39
00:02:50.495 --> 00:02:54.938
It was there one day where he spotted the obliging person with whom he had talked in Suez.

40
00:02:54.958 --> 00:03:02.637
"If I am not mistaken," Passepartout said, approaching Detective Fix, "you are the gentleman who kindly helped me

41
00:03:02.697 --> 00:03:03.238
in Suez."

42
00:03:03.258 --> 00:03:06.278
"Ah! I recognize you.

43
00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:11.780
Yes, you are the servant of the unusual Englishman," said Fix with a smile.

44
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.481
"Exactly, monsieur," said Passepartout.

45
00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:18.542
"Fix, my name is Fix."

46
00:03:18.562 --> 00:03:22.183
"Monsieur Fix, I am charmed to meet you.

47
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:24.124
Where are you bound?"

48
00:03:24.524 --> 00:03:27.644
asked Passepartout.

49
00:03:27.664 --> 00:03:29.845
"Like you, to Bombay," replied Fix.

50
00:03:29.865 --> 00:03:36.727
"Splendid. I have just finished my lunch," said Passepartout, as he reached into his pocket and pulled out

51
00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:37.967
his large silver watch.

52
00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:40.548
"It is now one o'clock.

53
00:03:41.648 --> 00:03:43.449
Would you like to walk around the deck with me?"

54
00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:45.849
"Yes, I would.

55
00:03:46.429 --> 00:03:49.030
But your watch does not tell the right time, sir.

56
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.431
It is slow," said Fix.

57
00:03:51.451 --> 00:03:53.151
"My watch?

58
00:03:53.731 --> 00:03:56.644
It is a family watch," said Passepartout.

59
00:03:56.664 --> 00:03:58.525
"It has come down to me from my great-grandfather!

60
00:03:59.506 --> 00:04:01.147
It doesn't vary five minutes in a year.

61
00:04:02.087 --> 00:04:03.128
It's a perfect timepiece."

62
00:04:04.568 --> 00:04:06.129
"I see," said Fix.

63
00:04:06.149 --> 00:04:12.413
"I believe you have kept London time, which is two hours behind that of the Suez.

64
00:04:13.913 --> 00:04:16.635
You should regulate your watch at noon in each country."

65
00:04:18.276 --> 00:04:19.296
"Regulate my watch?"

66
00:04:19.776 --> 00:04:20.777
asked Passepartout.

67
00:04:20.797 --> 00:04:25.960
"Never!" "Well, then, it will not agree with the sun," said Fix.

68
00:04:26.836 --> 00:04:28.697
"That is a problem for the sun, monsieur!"

69
00:04:29.878 --> 00:04:33.701
And with a defiant gesture, Passepartout returned his watch to his pocket.

70
00:04:35.422 --> 00:04:40.266
Fix decided to change the subject and in the most casual tone asked, "Is Mr.

71
00:04:40.306 --> 00:04:41.626
Fogg getting on well?"

72
00:04:41.646 --> 00:04:44.609
"Quite well," said Passepartout.

73
00:04:44.629 --> 00:04:45.829
"And I am too.

74
00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.250
It must be the sea air.

75
00:04:47.611 --> 00:04:49.232
I am eating like a famished giant."

76
00:04:50.713 --> 00:04:54.956
Fix smiled, and their amiable conversation continued with innocent topics.

77
00:04:56.556 --> 00:05:01.059
During the crossing Fix and Passepartout got into the habit of meeting and chatting regularly.

78
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.963
Fix often offered Passepartout a whiskey or beer in the steamer's bar, and Passepartout never failed to accept

79
00:05:09.003 --> 00:05:09.824
his generosity.

80
00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:14.286
The Mongolia was due in Bombay on October 22.

81
00:05:14.866 --> 00:05:16.587
But it arrived on October 20.

82
00:05:18.468 --> 00:05:18.929
So Mr.

83
00:05:18.969 --> 00:05:24.232
Fogg had gained two days, which he calmly entered in his travel journal after paying the captain his

84
00:05:24.312 --> 00:05:28.701
reward. Fix, however, was not as happy.

85
00:05:30.301 --> 00:05:35.102
As soon as the ship docked, Fix hastily made his way to the local police station in search

86
00:05:35.142 --> 00:05:36.263
of the warrant for Fogg.

87
00:05:38.163 --> 00:05:42.805
But being two days ahead of schedule, he knew the chances of finding it waiting for him were

88
00:05:42.865 --> 00:05:48.586
slim. As you just read, Mr.

89
00:05:48.626 --> 00:05:50.527
Fogg and I arrived in Bombay, India.

90
00:05:51.987 --> 00:05:54.368
Now you might be wondering why British police were in India.

91
00:05:56.052 --> 00:05:58.193
That's because India is a British colony.

92
00:05:59.453 --> 00:06:01.974
A colony is a country controlled by another country.

93
00:06:03.374 --> 00:06:07.636
Many powerful European countries have colonies in the Americas, Asia, and Africa.

94
00:06:08.916 --> 00:06:12.918
The main reason Britain has established colonies is so it can profit through trade.

95
00:06:14.538 --> 00:06:19.500
India has many spices, teas, and grains that are sold in Europe for six-thousand times the price they

96
00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:20.260
cost in India!

97
00:06:21.448 --> 00:06:26.492
In fact, India is so profitable, it's called the "jewel in the crown" of the British Empire.

98
00:06:27.573 --> 00:06:31.776
There has been a lot of resistance to British rule in India; many Indians are very poor.

99
00:06:32.537 --> 00:06:35.659
But Britain maintains its control with its very powerful army.
